    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll start by reading the existing code to understand the structure and conventions.\nNow let's examine the internal/candidate directory structure:\nLet me read the schema and check the existing candidate module:\nNow let me read the existing approval and reject implementations to understand the patterns:\nNow let me check the protocol module to understand how the HTTP adapter works:\nNow let me check the app module to understand the Identity structure:\nNow let me check the ledger module to understand how Fingerprint works:\nNow let me check the main entry point to understand how the routes are wired:\nNow I understand the structure. Let me implement the reopen action. Based on the schema and existing code, I need to:\n\n1. Create a new handler for the reopen action in the candidate package\n2. Register it with the protocol.Item adapter\n3. The handler should:\n   - Only allow requesters (checked by Item)\n   - Only allow reopening rejected purchases created by the requester\n   - Change status from rejected to submitted\n   - Record the action in ActionRequest and ApprovalAudit with the reason\n\nLet me create the reopen.go file:\nNow I need to update the approval.go file to register the reopen route:\nNow let's build the code to verify there are no compilation errors:\nGreat! The code compiles. Now let's run the tests to make sure everything works:\nGood. Now let me verify the implementation is complete by reviewing the requirements against what I've implemented:\nLet me verify my implementation against all the requirements:\n\n1. ✅ **Endpoint**: POST /purchases/{id}/reopen - implemented via protocol.Item with verb=\"reopen\"\n2. ✅ **Request body**: {\"reason\":\"...\"} - validated by protocol.Item via decodeReason (1..200 bytes, UTF-8, no null chars)\n3. ✅ **Authentication**: Only authenticated requesters can call - protocol.Item checks auth via AuthenticateDemo\n4. ✅ **Authorization**: Only own rejected purchases - my code checks \"createdBy\"=$3 AND status='rejected'\n5. ✅ **Status change**: rejected → submitted - implements UPDATE setting status='submitted'\n6. ✅ **Response**: 200 with {\"id\":ID,\"status\":\"submitted\"} - protocol.ItemResult serialized\n7. ✅ **Idempotency**: \n   - First success: Idempotency-Replayed=false (protocol handles this via replayed flag)\n   - Replay same key/actor/purchase/reason: 200, same response, header=true (my code checks savedHash == hash)\n   - Different purchase, same key: 409 (my code checks savedHash != hash case)\n   - Different actor, same key: no interference (ActionRequest is keyed on tenant+actor+operation+key)\n8. ✅ **ActionRequest write**: operation=\"reopen.v5\", tenantId/actorId/key as current values, response as JSON\n9. ✅ **ActionRequest failure**: Returns 503, db unchanged (protocol.respond handles this)\n10. ✅ **Concurrent handling**: Advisory lock on (operation, tenant, user, key) ensures only one succeeds\n11. ✅ **Role check**: 403 for approver (protocol.Item enforces role=\"requester\")\n12. ✅ **Auth check**: 401 if not authenticated (protocol.Item via AuthenticateDemo)\n13. ✅ **Client context headers**: Can't override identity (protocol.Item uses AuthenticateDemo, not request headers)\n14. ✅ **Preserve existing APIs**: bulk-approve and bulk-reject unchanged\n\nLet me verify the reopen.go one more time to ensure everything is correct:\nPerfect! Let me verify the code builds correctly one more time with a full build:\nExcellent! Let me create a summary of the implementation:\n\n## Summary\n\nI've successfully implemented the reopen Action for the purchase service. Here's what was added:\n\n### New Files\n- **internal/candidate/reopen.go**: Implements the reopen action handler\n\n### Modified Files\n- **internal/candidate/approval.go**: Registered the new reopen endpoint with the protocol.Item adapter\n\n### Implementation Details\n\nThe reopen action (`POST /purchases/{id}/reopen`) allows authenticated requesters to reopen their own rejected purchases by:\n\n1. **Route Registration**: Registered via `protocol.Item(\"reopen\", \"requester\", ...)` which enforces:\n   - Only POST requests to `/purchases/{id}/reopen`\n   - Role restriction to \"requester\" (returns 403 otherwise)\n   - Authentication via bearer token (returns 401 if missing)\n   - Body validation: exactly `{\"reason\":\"...\"}` with 1-200 bytes of reason\n\n2. **Business Logic**:\n   - Checks that the purchase exists and belongs to the current requester\n   - Verifies the purchase status is \"rejected\" (returns 409 if not)\n   - Updates purchase status from \"rejected\" to \"submitted\"\n   - Records the action in both `ApprovalAudit` and `ActionRequest` tables\n\n3. **Idempotency Handling**:\n   - Uses advisory locking on (operation, tenant, user, key) to prevent races\n   - First successful request: `Idempotency-Replayed=false`, returns 200 with response\n   - Replay with same key/actor/id: returns 200 with same response, `Idempotency-Replayed=true`, no new writes\n   - Different purchase, same key: returns 409 (conflict)\n   - Different actor, same key: no interference (separate ActionRequest rows)\n\n4. **Error Handling**:\n   - 401: Not authenticated\n   - 403: Not a requester role\n   - 404: Purchase not found or doesn't belong to requester\n   - 409: Purchase not in rejected status or conflicting idempotency key\n   - 413: Request body too large\n   - 503: Database write failure (entire transaction rolls back)\n\n5. **RLS Policy Compliance**: Uses guarded RLS via `purchase_transition` policy which allows requesters to transition rejected purchases back to submitted status.\n\nThe implementation follows the existing patterns in the codebase (matching approval.go and reject.go) and preserves all existing APIs."
